                        "entry_id": "87e62e62-e5b5-3293-b000-6b78734c4c03",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The office of, or landed estate granted to, a senator, especially in France under the consulate and First French Empire."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "20b4e01a-432c-4f80-9467-a00c16172b92",
                                "sense_id": "9afb2d3a-00a6-305f-be59-3f41a0b4c8dd",
                                "text": "Made a Senator, he received the senatory of Aix. Another \"Jacobin,\" the Senator Monge, was given the senatory of Liège. Démeunier exhibited signs of independence; he was given the senatory of Toulouse."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "155b51d2-1f54-429c-8e41-5ef7ae655c1d",
                                "sense_id": "9afb2d3a-00a6-305f-be59-3f41a0b4c8dd",
                                "text": "The 1806 private \"Agronomic travels in the senatory of Dijon\" by Francois de Neufchateau (himself a former minister and president of the Society and hence no ordinary savant) had given way by 1813 to memoirs on cotton cultivation in France ..."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d6b42918-de7f-4413-b59a-7b6f03ba80ef",
                                "sense_id": "9afb2d3a-00a6-305f-be59-3f41a0b4c8dd",
                                "text": "1994, Eric Anderson Arnold, A Documentary Survey of Napoleonic France, University Press of Amer\nOn Senatories\n1) There will be a Senatory for each district of the Court of Appeals.\n2) Each Senatory will be endowed with a house and an annual income, drawn from the national domain, of 20,000 to 25,000 francs."
                            }
                        ]
